<p>I used to keep a list of projects. It was long, ambitious, and mostly a source of guilt. Every item was a reminder of the person I was not yet. Now I keep a shelf of unfinished ideas. Some are sketches. Some are sentences. Some are just names.</p>

<p>An unfinished idea is not a failure. It is a seed. It needs the right weather, which is usually another idea, a conversation, or a season of your life that has not arrived yet.</p>

<p>The problem with lists is that they flatten everything into tasks. A task can be finished. A seed cannot. If you treat every spark as an obligation, you will eventually stop allowing yourself to spark.</p>

<ul>
  <li>Keep a place for fragments that does not demand completion.</li>
  <li>Revisit the shelf every few months, not every day.</li>
  <li>Let some ideas expire without turning it into a moral failure.</li>
</ul>

<p>The work does not have to justify itself by becoming a product. Some of it is just practice. Some of it is compost. The shelf is not a backlog; it is a garden.</p>
